{"records":[{"id":"m93sk9n767","title":"OV_patient_longitudinal_CNA_samples","description":"Copy number profiles and liquidCNA algorithm output for patient samples presented in \"Adaptive therapy achieves long-term control of chemotherapy resistance in high grade ovarian cancer\". See ReadMe.txt for explanation of file contents.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-08T12:50:06.77Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m93sk9n767","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m93sk9n767"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Eszter","last_name":"Lakatos","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wpw7mstpp8","title":"Gene sequencing of a rare mutant lung cancer","description":"This sequencing contains 73 genes associated with the occurrence and development of lung cancer, including important genes related to targeted therapy and drug resistance such as BRAF, ALK, RET, ROS1, MET, EGFR, KRAS, ERBB2 in the NCCN guidelines, as well as genes related to PD-1/PD-L1 immunotherapy such as MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2, POLE, and genes related to chemotherapy drug toxicity and efficacy.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-01T15:15:22.885Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wpw7mstpp8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wpw7mstpp8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":" FANGFANG ","last_name":"WANG","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9d5499gb8x","title":"In Vivo CRISPR Screens Identify E3 Ligase Cop1 as a Modulator of Macrophage Infiltration and Cancer Immunotherapy Target","description":"Despite remarkable clinical efficacy of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in cancer treatment, ICB benefits in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) remain limited. Through pooled in vivo CRISPR knockout (KO) screens in syngeneic TNBC mouse models, we found that deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells decreases the secretion of macrophage-associated chemokines, reduces tumor macrophage infiltration, enhances anti-tumor immunity, and strengthens ICB response. Transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics analyses revealed that Cop1 functions through proteasomal degradation of the C/ebpδ protein. Cop1 substrate Trib2 functions as a scaffold linking Cop1 and C/ebpδ, which leads to polyubiquitination of C/ebpδ. In addition, deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells stabilizes C/ebpδ to suppress the expression of macrophage chemoattractant genes. Our integrated approach implicates Cop1 as a target for improving cancer immunotherapy efficacy in TNBC by regulating chemokine secretion and macrophage infiltration in the tumor microenvironment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-24T14:43:28.246Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9d5499gb8x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9d5499gb8x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"xiaoqing","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bsjc7f3hck","title":"STING-dependent paracriny shapes apoptotic priming of breast tumors in response to  anti-mitotic treatment. Lohard et al.","description":"A fascinating but uncharacterized action of antimitotic chemotherapy is to collectively prime cancer cells to apoptotic mitochondrial outer membrane permeabilization (MOMP), while impacting only on cycling cell subsets. Here, we show that a proapoptotic secretory phenotype is induced by activation of cGAS/STING in cancer cells that are hit by antimitotic treatment, accumulate micronuclei and maintain mitochondrial integrity despite intrinsic apoptotic pressure. Organotypic cultures of primary human breast tumors and patient-derived xenografts sensitive to paclitaxel exhibit gene expression signatures typical of type I IFN and TNFα exposure. These cytokines induced by cGAS/STING activation trigger NOXA expression in neighboring cells and render them acutely sensitive to BCL-xL inhibition. cGAS/STING-dependent apoptotic effects are required for paclitaxel response in vivo, and they are amplified by sequential, but not synchronous, administration of BH3 mimetics. Thus anti-mitotic agents propagate apoptotic priming across heterogeneously sensitive cancer cells through cytosolic DNA sensing pathway-dependent extracellular signals, exploitable by delayed MOMP targeting.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-11-14T14:20:01.824Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bsjc7f3hck","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bsjc7f3hck"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Steven","last_name":"Lohard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fabien","last_name":"Gautier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laurent","last_name":"David","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sophie","last_name":"Barillé-Nion","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z2p776dhvf","title":"CD44 Overexpression as a Mediator of Drug Resistance in Oral Cancer: A Meta-Analysis Unveiling Molecular Underpinnings and Therapeutic Implications","description":"Background: Therapeutic resistance in oral cancer, with cancer stem cells (CSCs) playing a pivotal role, remains a major clinical challenge. CD44, a key CSC marker, has been implicated in multidrug resistance mechanisms through drug efflux, DNA repair, and anti-apoptotic pathways. This meta-analysis aims to systematically evaluate the association between CD44 expression and clinicopathological characteristics in oral cancer patients through analysis of controlled clinical studies.\nMethods: We systematically searched PubMed, EMBASE, Cochrane Library, Web of Science, CNKI, VIP, WanFang, and China Biomedical Literature Database from inception to September 2022. Controlled clinical studies examining CD44 overexpression in relation to clinicopathological characteristics and survival outcomes in oral cancer patients were included. Quality assessment was performed using the Newcastle-Ottawa Scale. Data were analyzed using RevMan 5.3 software. \nResults: Ten high-quality studies comprising 846 oral cancer patients were included. CD44 overexpression was significantly associated with advanced local tumor invasion (T category: OR: 1.38; 95% CI: 1.14-1.66; P<0.001; I²=25%). No significant associations were found with N category, histological grade, or vascular invasion. Pooled survival analysis was limited by heterogeneous reporting methods across studies.\nConclusion: CD44 overexpression serves as a molecular marker of therapeutic resistance in oral cancer, particularly associated with local tumor progression. These findings highlight CD44-targeted therapies as promising strategies to overcome treatment resistance. Future large-scale, multicenter trials are needed to validate CD44 isoforms as prognostic biomarkers and therapeutic targets.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-11T14:57:45.69Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z2p776dhvf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z2p776dhvf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Anqi","last_name":"Zheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shushu","last_name":"Miao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caiqiong","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Meta-Analysis","Oral Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vp8d9f8rff","title":"Tumor-derived miR200a inhibits metastasis via Wnt5a/IL-6/B7-H4 axis in ovarian cancer","description":"Wnt5a/IL-6/B7-H4 axis was involved in tumor-derived miR200a inhibited-metastasis in ovarian cancer. B7-H4 in ovarian cancer cells and macrophages both exerted anti-metastasis effects, suggesting the important role of B7-H4 in ovarian cancer.fu","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-02T17:44:10.655Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vp8d9f8rff","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vp8d9f8rff"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"He","last_name":"Fei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Medicine","Health Sciences","Oncology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ngttmkkhkn","title":"ALDOA-mediated metabolic reprogramming is a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization in cancer","description":"Ferroptosis presents great potential for cancer therapy, either alone or in combination with classical therapy. However, inducing ferroptosis by targeting canonical ferroptosis suppressors that directly inhibit lipid peroxidation non-selectively induces ferroptosis in both cancerous and normal cells, thereby limiting its therapeutic potential. In this study, we reveal that aldolase A (ALDOA) reprograms lipid metabolism to resist ferroptosis in cancer cells and identify ALDOA as a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization. Cancer cells with ALDOA suppression exhibit increased susceptibility to ferroptosis-a response less obvious in normal cells. Mechanistically, ALDOA depletion induces significant accumulation of fructose 1,6-bisphosphate in cancer cells, thereby enhancing autophagy-dependent degradation of phospholipid-modifying enzymes. These alterations increase the ratio of phospholipids containing pro-ferroptotic polyunsaturated fatty acids over anti-ferroptotic monounsaturated fatty acids, culminating in heightened ferroptosis sensitivity. Moreover, ALDOA inhibitors selectively promote ferroptosis in cancer cells, both in vitro and in vivo. Collectively, our findings reveal that ALDOA-mediated metabolic reprogramming is a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization in cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-27T01:37:28.511Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ngttmkkhkn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ngttmkkhkn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"pengqi","last_name":"wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"sheng","last_name":"ding","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Tsinghua-Peking Center for Life Sciences","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"Beijing Natural Science Foundation","grant_id":"JQ22016"},{"id":null,"name":"New Cornerstone Investigator Program","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"National Key R&D Program of China ","grant_id":"2022YFA1103704"}],"categories":["Lipidomics","Metabolomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fsn398v4pn","title":"letrozole plus anlotinib","description":"This is the original dataset from the clinical study investigating letrozole plus anlotinib as a chemotherapy-free regimen for ER-positive, platinum-resistant, recurrent epithelial ovarian cancer after failure of second-line therapy.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-18T18:48:35.369Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fsn398v4pn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fsn398v4pn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xipeng","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yizhi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiarui","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yiting","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hong","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuhui","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wenbin","last_name":"Guan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Ovarian Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pm5v6pwkkg","title":"Genome-wide screens implicate loss of cullin ring ligase 3 in persistent proliferation and genome instability in TP53 deficient cells","description":"TP53 deficiency is the most common alteration in cancer, however, alone is typically insufficient to drive tumorigenesis. To identify genes promoting tumorigenesis in combination with TP53 deficiency, we performed genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens coupled with proliferation and transformation assays in isogenic cell lines. Loss of several known tumor suppressor genes enhanced cellular proliferation and transformation. Surprisingly, loss neddylation pathway genes, promoted uncontrolled proliferation exclusively in TP53-deficient cells. CUL3 coupled with TP53 loss results in an oncogenic transcriptional program governed by the NFκB, AP-1 and TGF-β pathways. This program maintains persistent cellular proliferation, induces partial epithelial to mesenchymal transition, increases DNA damage, genomic instability, and the rate chromosomal rearrangements. Our findings reveal CUL3 loss as a key event stimulating persistent proliferation in TP53 deficient cells. These findings may be clinically relevant, since TP53-CUL3 deficient cells are highly sensitive to ATM inhibition, exposing a vulnerability that could be exploited for cancer treatment. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T17:31:10.704Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pm5v6pwkkg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pm5v6pwkkg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexandros","last_name":"Drainas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rgcjv65433","title":"IF and chemotherapy in ovarian cancer","description":"IF and chemotherapy","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-14T14:22:47.149Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rgcjv65433","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rgcjv65433"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Francesca","last_name":"Ricci","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Prism"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":135700,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":157,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":135700,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":135010,"restricted":67,"embargoed":623},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2279},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1335},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":649},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":473},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":447},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":427},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":410},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":383},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":323},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":270}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":133067},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1901},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":136},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":77},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":64},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":56},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":40},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":867},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":767},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":519},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":405},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}